# Sub-PR design discipline

Cite this rule explicitly when scoping or reviewing a multi-round
per-trait PR: consensus behavior, an FFI boundary, or an inherited
reference port. Single-commit additions, doc-only changes, and refactors
outside that scope do not invoke it. The injection model is the same as
`07-consensus-atomic-cutovers.mdc`.

Each discipline below is a rejection with reopening criteria and a
re-evaluation shape (`21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc`). Stable IDs
(`R0-D#`, `R3-D1`, `R5-D1`) are audit-trail anchors. Do not rename them.

Every numeric claim in a plan — constants, budgets, line counts, commit
counts — is verified at pre-flight. **B6** checks a constant against the
cited source line. **B9** runs the prescribed bench and reconciles the
threshold against the measurement. A budget written from inherited-code
intuition, without measuring the Rust implementation on the actual
workload, is the failure B9 exists for.

## Scope

Applies when work is split across short-lived PRs with design rounds
before implementation, especially consensus, FFI, or a reference port.
Trait-specific plan docs still carry the substrate enumeration. This
rule is the process those docs follow.

## Pre-flight (Round 0)

Between design closure and production code:

1. **Substrate re-check.** Re-read the source, call graph, and
   conventions each closed disposition cites, at the audit pin.
2. **Artifact execution.** Run the tests, benches, and generators the
   plan prescribes. Reconcile measurements against budgets before the
   PR opens.

Pre-flight is not a redesign. Record findings in the plan's status block
and a sibling audit trail (`docs/design/<TRAIT>_AUDIT.md`).

**Halt.** No implementation commit lands on a branch whose governing
design doc names an undischarged pre-flight pass. If the plan says the
sequence proceeds after the pass, the pass is run and recorded before
the first production commit. Not concurrently, and not retroactively.
When about to commit against an undischarged named pass, halt and run
it. A breach that has already happened is recorded in `docs/FOLLOWUPS.md`
as a breach, never cited as precedent.

Design closure means closure plus active defense against named attacker
objectives, not per-finding whack-a-mole.

## Part A — design rounds

Apply at round closure and when scoping the next sub-PR.

### A1. Function-body replacement

Freeze the signature, the caller state fields, and the dispatch inputs.
Land a NOP or `unimplemented!()` body. The next sub-PR replaces the body
in place.

Reject a trait-plus-stub, and reject signature churn between "extract
the surface" and "implement the surface." A trait with one implementation
is debt (`21-reversion-clause-discipline.mdc`).

Reopen the frozen signature only when a downstream benchmark or
threat-model review shows the frozen shape cannot meet a load-bearing
budget, and profiling attributes the shortfall to that shape rather than
to a missing implementation. The re-evaluation is a design-round entry
naming the frozen surface, the evidence, and the amended contract, then
a separate commit sequence (`90-commits.mdc`).

### A2. Audit against the code

Plan-doc audit tables cite pinned line ranges. The load-bearing step is
reading those lines, not producing a plausible table. A table without
citations is a claim that no audit happened.

The discipline does not relax on a consensus-critical port. An uncited
table is tolerable only for a non-consensus internal refactor whose
reviewer-map documents zero consensus-affecting consumers
(`07-consensus-atomic-cutovers.mdc` criterion 4).

A reviewer spot-checks the cited file at the pinned commit. The row
fails when the cited lines do not support the disposition. Every row
names `path:N–M` at the audit pin.

The tell: a field the table treats as state is a local alias in the
reference, or a frequency ladder makes a branch unreachable. "The table
looks plausible" and "we caught one bug, audit done" are both the
failure. The table is output. Reading the source is the audit.

### A3. Threat-model addenda

Late in the design rounds, after the design is stable and before
closure, pass 3–6 named attacker objectives. Route each finding to
in-scope absorption, a discipline note, or a named downstream
forward-action.

Skip the round only when the reviewer-map shows zero cryptographic,
consensus, or untrusted-input surface. If such a surface appears later,
reopen with a fresh objective enumeration. A finding with no route is
lost. Closure without the adversarial pass is freeze-then-whack-a-mole.

### A4. Boundary changes carry a reversion clause

A scope move between sub-PRs ("was 2e, now 2c") records the substrate
reason and the reopening criteria. Cite rule 21 for the three-part
shape. Do not restate it.

A deferred boundary reopens when a named production caller or a
consensus event requires the abstracted surface now. Test convenience
does not reopen it. A scope move updates the parent plan's phase list
in the same change.

A PR whose scope was ruled carries the ruled row or phase range in its
body. A commit that lands outside that range updates the line or
re-rules the split in the same turn, never later. The body is the one
surface a reviewer reads before the diff, so it is where the branch is
checked against the ruling; a ruling that lives only in the plan doc is
checked by nobody at commit time.

### A5. Forward actions have two anchors

Every finding is absorbed now or carried to a named PR scaffold, and
the decision is recorded in both the source doc and the target doc.
"The next phase will figure it out" is not a carry. A carry names the
phase and the disposition id. Inline absorption instead of a carry is
allowed only when the downstream PR is not yet scaffolded, the work is
about a hundred lines or less, and it has zero consensus coupling to
that phase — recorded, with why inline was cheaper.

## Part B — pre-flight disciplines

Apply before production commits. IDs `R0-D5`–`R0-D12` stay stable.

### B1. Test fidelity versus dominant cost (`R0-D5`)

Reject a test-only API justified by per-test savings when the build
dominates the wall clock, or when the shortcut breaks the test's
meaning. The test calls the real constructor. Reintroduce a fast path
only when measurement shows it is load-bearing against the dominant
cost (CI execution, not a local build) and the fidelity claim remains
true.

### B2. Tests use the actual API (`R0-D6`)

Reject widening `pub`, adding a test-only accessor, or relaxing
visibility so an integration test can read internal state. A new `pub`
test-only API reopens only with a named production caller in the same
PR. Otherwise move the test next to the code (`src/*.rs`, `mod tests`)
and use `include_bytes!` fixtures.

### B3. Visibility follows the call graph (`R0-D7`)

Reject `pub(crate)` or `pub` for a helper with no direct production
caller. Transitive use through another entry point is not that caller.
B2 is widening the API for tests. B3 is promoting a helper nobody calls
directly. Both reject surface without a production call graph.

### B4. Results over shape (`R0-D8`)

Reject a 1:1 port of reference method counts, class shapes, or
intermediate fields when the target language produces the same bytes
with cleaner ownership. Reopen a literal shape port only when fusion
provably changes consensus bytes, shown with a spec vector. Name the C
shape and the Rust shape side by side.

### B5. Every commit builds (`R0-D8` sibling)

Reject `include_bytes!` of a fixture before the commit that generates
it, and any commit sequence that breaks `cargo build` at an intermediate
SHA. A fixture-before-generator sequence is allowed only when the
fixtures are already checked in from a prior phase and the commit table
cites their provenance. New vectors wait for the generator commit.
Property tests that need no fixture can land earlier.

### B6. Numeric constants (`R0-D9`)

A constant in a plan table is read off the cited substrate line at the
audit pin. Adjacent definitions get conflated (`PROGRAM_ITERATIONS`
versus `PROGRAM_SIZE`). B6 is source-line verification. B9 is
measurement. A citation can be the right line and the value column
still wrong. Always check both. A wrong constant that already landed is
a separate fix-up commit.

### B7. Implicit state lost in a fusion (`R0-D10`)

Reject fusing two reference storage shapes into one Rust field without
explicit assertions for behaviour that fell out of the reference's
separate lifetimes: default init, per-scope reset. Omit the explicit
reset only with proof that the Rust type's default or drop matches the
reference for every entry point, including nested calls the tests do
not cover. Name the C semantics and reset at the equivalent transition.

### B8. Byte identity versus result equivalence (`R0-D11`)

Require byte-identical serialization only when a downstream consumer
hashes or persists that intermediate representation in consensus.
Otherwise result-equivalence is the spec. When the Rust path is
spec-faithful and the C artifact is an optimization, fix the generator
or the test oracle, not the port.

### B9. Measure the budget (`R0-D12`)

Reject an absolute PR-gate threshold set from an estimate, and reject
treating a plan budget as a gate when the bench measures a wider
surface than the budget text describes. Revise a threshold when an
optimization lands with a measured delta, when amortization changes
what the budget measures, or when a downstream phase isolates the
bounded portion. Record the measurement, the threshold, and the
reopening criteria. A gate labeled in microseconds that benches a
full hash is this failure.

## Review-round denominator

A review round records what it examined and did not find, separately
from where it did not look. A findings list is a numerator. Without
the denominator, a clean surface and an unexamined one read the same
(`47-gate-subject-assertion.mdc`, `19-validation-surface-discipline.mdc`).

Close every round with a short paragraph naming the surfaces examined
that yielded nothing. Name surfaces, not reassurances. A surface nobody
examined is the next round's scope, not this round's clean list.

A figure carries its scope and its unit in the same sentence. "Five
sites" is not a claim until it says five of what, in which file. Counts
of different units are not comparable.

Two corollaries:

1. A fix can hide the next question. When a round closes a hole, ask
   what that hole was making visible.
2. A ruling relayed from someone else is not that person's signature.
   A round run on relayed rulings says so. If the words were never
   said, the section is not closed.

## When a reviewer sees a violation

An uncited audit row, a test-only `pub` widener, or a scope move
without a reversion clause is fixed in the process artifact. Do not
fold an unrelated implementation fix into that PR
(`15-deletion-and-debt.mdc`).

